WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:04.962
(inspirational music)

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.220
(thunder rolls)

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.433
- [Sebastián] Iberá was a place
that was degraded by humans.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.860
And it's a place that is
being recovered by humans.

00:00:23.416 --> 00:00:27.000
It's an incredible example
of what we can achieve

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.120
if we have the decision
of restoring an ecosystem

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:30.970
on a large scale.

00:00:35.372 --> 00:00:38.372
(hooves thundering)

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.356
(birds singing)

00:00:53.356 --> 00:00:55.532
(crickets chirping)

00:00:55.532 --> 00:00:58.282
(girth creaking)

00:01:01.153 --> 00:01:03.736
(hand patting)

00:01:06.028 --> 00:01:07.530
(grass rustling)

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.770
My job is related mostly
to bring back the species

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.790
that went extinct in these
different national parks

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.339
in which we are working.

00:01:15.339 --> 00:01:18.495
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:19.620
(gentle melancholy music)

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.273
The biggest project is
based here in Iberá...

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.680
A big project sometimes so complicated

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.713
that you cannot do it by yourself.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.800
In Rewilding Argentina we developed a team

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.930
that is an incredible group of people.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.223
They are passionate for their work.

00:01:43.657 --> 00:01:46.407
(static buzzing)

00:01:50.654 --> 00:01:53.478
The very first species that
we reintroduced here in Iberá

00:01:53.478 --> 00:01:55.073
was the giant anteater.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.030
Every animal that we
release has a transmitter.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.796
That way we can follow the
animal after it's been released.

00:02:07.796 --> 00:02:12.796
(transmitter buzzing)
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:13.342 --> 00:02:15.240
So the guys that monitor them

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.320
can look at them several times a week,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.569
and see how they are adapting
to their new environment.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:24.393
(transmitter beeping)

00:02:24.393 --> 00:02:27.393
(branches cracking)

00:02:29.076 --> 00:02:32.993
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:35.452 --> 00:02:38.035
(man whistles)

00:02:39.656 --> 00:02:43.573
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:52.012 --> 00:02:54.679
(birds singing)

00:02:55.519 --> 00:02:59.436
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:04.597 --> 00:03:08.260
(branches cracking)

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.313
They are doing incredibly
well by their own.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.816
So the anteater is a
story of success, really.

00:03:17.816 --> 00:03:20.697
(gentle guitar music)

00:03:20.697 --> 00:03:23.720
We are in the northeastern
part of Argentina,

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.463
in a province called Corrientes,
in the Iberá National Park.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.760
It's one of the biggest
wetlands of South America.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.730
It's a very productive ecosystem.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:39.130
It's full of mammals,
and birds, and reptiles,

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:42.490
and also a lot of
species that went extinct

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.083
in the last century.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.440
There were a lot of
hunters inside the marsh.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:52.250
They started to hunt
birds to get the feathers.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:55.680
And they then turned to
other species like caimans,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.253
capybaras, jaguars, to get their skins.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:04.160
Our vision is to try to help reverse

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.356
the biodiversity extinction
crisis that we are facing.

00:04:07.356 --> 00:04:08.520
(water splashing)

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:13.520
How we do that in Argentina
is by protecting what is left.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.020
That's why we create big
national parks like Iberá.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:21.210
And we restore the land
mostly by reintroducing

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.630
keystone species that went extinct

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.373
that are crucial for the
ecosystem functioning.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.840
We are working in
different places in Iberá.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.300
So far there are six different places

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.493
where we are reintroducing species.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.490
And then we develop economic activity

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.900
for the local people,
so they can get benefits

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.880
from that park that is
going to be created,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.950
and the wildlife that we
are starting to recover.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.540
Some of the species that
now are very common,

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.680
those ones they could recover.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.010
But others could never
recover by themselves.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:05.210
(car engine rumbling)

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.620
For us to start the project

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.673
we have to bring animals from captivity.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.600
So now we are heading to
one of the quarantines

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.660
that we have for the project.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.190
In this quarantine we also have

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:21.530
a giant anteater rescue center.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.450
Every year several animals
arrive to the center.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.920
Most of them are orphans whose mother

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.023
have been killed by poachers.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.150
All of the animals that
come from captivity

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:38.021
start their journey in the
project in the quarantine.

00:05:38.021 --> 00:05:40.166
(hand brake creaks)

00:05:40.166 --> 00:05:43.029
(door opens)

00:05:43.029 --> 00:05:45.170
(door closes)

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:46.003
Ali.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.359
(latch clicks)

00:05:49.359 --> 00:05:53.276
(speaking in foreign language)

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.400
- [Sebastián As Voiceover] Ali
is an Argentinian biologist

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.760
and she is the one that takes
care of all these animals

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.943
until they are ready to go
to the field to be released.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.510
Usually when the animals
arrive, they are very small.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.190
They need to be fed with special milk...

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.413
several times a day.

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:19.850
(gentle music)

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:40.617
(speaking in foreign language)

00:07:04.497 --> 00:07:06.747
(laughing)

00:07:16.591 --> 00:07:21.003
(speaking in foreign language)

00:07:21.003 --> 00:07:22.730
When they are big enough

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:24.993
to carry a harness with a transmitter...

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.183
we can release them in the wild.

00:07:30.875 --> 00:07:33.708
(tongue slurping)

00:07:42.063 --> 00:07:44.313
(sniffing)

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.000
The giant otter is another species

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.270
that went completely extinct in Argentina,

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:56.840
because of hunting, because
we destroyed their habitats

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.151
through logging.

00:07:58.151 --> 00:07:59.121
(keys jingle)

00:07:59.121 --> 00:08:01.871
(locks thudding)

00:08:04.019 --> 00:08:04.960
(water splashing)

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:05.793
It's the first time that our

00:08:05.793 --> 00:08:07.830
reintroduction project

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.980
is run for giant otters.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.230
But the thing is that there are no otters

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.904
in captivity in Argentina.

00:08:15.904 --> 00:08:19.110
(speaking in foreign language)

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:23.075
So Coco is the first male of the project.

00:08:23.075 --> 00:08:26.510
(door scraping)

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.773
He's still in the quarantine.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:33.343
He came from a zoo in Denmark.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:36.380
And once he finishes the quarantine,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:41.130
we will bring him to the Iberá
park to a pre-release pen...

00:08:43.666 --> 00:08:46.190
where a female that also came from Europe

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.913
is waiting for him. That's Alondra.

00:08:51.283 --> 00:08:53.110
Coco and Alondra, they
don't know each other.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:56.250
So there's a long way for them to learn

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.610
how to raise the cubs.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:01.410
And once they are ready, we will be able

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.263
to release them in the wild.

00:09:07.657 --> 00:09:10.324
(pole scraping)

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:13.870
With the macaws we have the most hard work

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:16.270
for rehabilitating those animals.

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:20.690
Because most of the macaws
that we bring to the project,

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:23.139
they come from zoos or
wildlife rescue centers...

00:09:23.139 --> 00:09:24.880
(whistle blows)

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:27.050
where they spend many years of their lives

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.950
in small cages without
the possibility of flying.

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:34.030
You not only have to change their diet,

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:36.643
but we also have to teach them how to fly.

00:09:36.643 --> 00:09:39.054
(whistle blows)

00:09:39.054 --> 00:09:41.955
(wings flapping)

00:09:41.955 --> 00:09:44.680
(macaw chattering)

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.840
It can be up to one, two
years for each individual,

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.780
in which we train these animals
that come from captivity

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:54.193
to survive in the wild.

00:09:56.638 --> 00:09:59.471
(macaw squawking)

00:10:00.808 --> 00:10:03.475
(birds singing)

00:10:07.731 --> 00:10:08.837
(chickens clucking)

00:10:08.837 --> 00:10:12.370
All this park creation
process and rewilding process

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:16.670
cannot be done without the
support of the local communities.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:20.587
(speaking in foreign language)

00:10:26.387 --> 00:10:28.350
- [Sebastián As Voiceover]
Pellegrini is a small town

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:31.890
by the Iberá park that had
an economy that was based

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:36.890
mostly in forestry, cattle
ranching, and rice fields.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:41.080
Now, since the creation
of the provincial reserve,

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:44.100
their main economic
activity is ecotourism.

00:10:46.547 --> 00:10:50.464
(speaking in foreign language)

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:13.510
- People become proud of what they have,

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:17.490
and now young people choose
to stay in their hometown, and

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:22.387
they start to run ecotourism
businesses as local guides.

00:11:22.387 --> 00:11:25.220
(uplifting music)

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:37.310
They use their knowledge
and they become very proud

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:39.750
that people come from
different parts of the world

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.163
to see what they know.

00:11:43.874 --> 00:11:47.791
(speaking in foreign language)

00:12:07.463 --> 00:12:10.296
(water splashing)

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.840
- 30 or 40 years ago, we had the idea

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.710
that the best thing that you
can do to preserve a species

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:32.491
is not touch anything.

00:12:32.491 --> 00:12:36.060
(birds chirping)

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:38.930
Now we say that the best
thing that you can do,

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:41.950
and sometimes the only
thing that you can do,

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.253
is to be very active.

00:12:45.547 --> 00:12:50.280
(speaking in foreign language)

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.930
So far we've been successful reintroducing

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:56.270
some of the species here,
like the giant anteater,

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:59.740
the pampas deer...But we are just starting

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:02.290
with the top predators, like the jaguar,

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:05.142
so there's a lot of work to do here.

00:13:05.142 --> 00:13:06.554
(crickets chirping)

00:13:06.554 --> 00:13:08.356
(speaking in foreign language)

00:13:08.356 --> 00:13:11.024
(radio static buzzes)

00:13:11.024 --> 00:13:14.941
(speaking in foreign language)

00:13:17.915 --> 00:13:21.082
(radio static buzzes)

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.345
The jaguar reintroduction project

00:13:26.345 --> 00:13:31.345
is the most complicated and
ambitious project that we have.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.700
We built a very big reintroduction center

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:36.680
with very huge pens.

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:41.023
If you go there, it looks like
a Jurassic Park for jaguars.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:46.210
So far, we already have five
animals at this reintroduction

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:50.083
center that we think are
ready to be released.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:53.860
We hope that during this year,

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:57.800
we will have the first
jaguars living free in Iberá,

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:01.160
and that will be like the
coronation (laughs) of the

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:05.163
rewilding project, because it
has never been done before.

00:14:06.659 --> 00:14:09.752
(gravel crunching)

00:14:09.752 --> 00:14:12.760
We have to move one of the
female jaguars, Mariuá,

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:17.440
to the biggest enclosure of
the reintroduction center.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.120
It's a very big step for the project

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:23.320
because it's almost
free of our management,

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:26.640
so it's like being already living free.

00:14:28.962 --> 00:14:32.879
(speaking in foreign language)

00:14:45.218 --> 00:14:49.135
(speaking in foreign language)

00:15:08.493 --> 00:15:11.160
(gate rattling)

00:15:12.939 --> 00:15:15.949
(uplifting music)

00:15:15.949 --> 00:15:18.400
- Rewilding is not only
about bringing back

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:20.877
the keystone species that went extinct.

00:15:28.355 --> 00:15:31.813
(people chatting)

00:15:31.813 --> 00:15:35.047
(ramps thudding)

00:15:35.047 --> 00:15:39.610
Rewilding is a proactive
conservation action

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:43.903
that brings joy to your soul. (laughs)

00:15:45.904 --> 00:15:48.892
(gate clanking)

00:15:48.892 --> 00:15:51.559
(paws thudding)

00:15:54.801 --> 00:15:57.276
(speaking in foreign language)

00:15:57.276 --> 00:15:59.439
(water splashing)

00:15:59.439 --> 00:16:01.617
(otters squeaking)

00:16:01.617 --> 00:16:04.617
When I see a group of macaws flying,

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:08.750
what you see is all
the effort that you put

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:11.696
so that these birds can make it.

00:16:11.696 --> 00:16:13.595
(whistle blows)

00:16:13.595 --> 00:16:16.345
(wings flapping)

00:16:17.585 --> 00:16:19.227
(branch thudding)

00:16:19.227 --> 00:16:22.210
(whistle blows)

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:27.050
It's many months of work
with any individual.

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:29.950
So it's not just two
macaws flying (chuckles),

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:34.383
but also a lot of history
behind those incredible colors.

00:16:37.873 --> 00:16:40.348
(macaw squawking)

00:16:40.348 --> 00:16:42.530
(wings flapping)

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.260
We don't live in cities.
(woman singing)

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:46.870
We live in the place where we are working.

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:49.943
We become neighbors of the local people.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.490
They are the ones that
can tell you the stories

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.921
about the animals, about the places.

00:16:55.921 --> 00:16:58.587
(birds chirping)

00:16:58.587 --> 00:17:02.504
(speaking in foreign language)

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:07.650
As a team, we are really
proud that we are developing

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:11.890
a good example, that we
don't need to destroy nature

00:17:11.890 --> 00:17:14.193
to have a better life quality.

00:17:16.123 --> 00:17:18.620
(speaking in foreign language)

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:22.950
You just feel a great satisfaction
that we are fixing, as a

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:27.493
species, most of the bad
things that we did in the past.

00:17:32.411 --> 00:17:37.360
(speaking in foreign language over radio)

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:40.086
(team applauding)

00:17:40.086 --> 00:17:43.253
(inspirational music)

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:56.557
(speaking in foreign language)

00:18:00.555 --> 00:18:03.890
What I always think is, if
we can reintroduce jaguars

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:06.103
in a place that they went extinct...

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:10.490
what else we can not do?

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:13.540
I think, (laughs) it's
like we can do everything.

00:18:14.763 --> 00:18:16.715
(speaking in foreign language)

00:18:16.715 --> 00:18:18.965
(laughing)

00:18:38.238 --> 00:18:41.488
(melodic guitar music)

00:18:42.992 --> 00:18:46.825
(singing in foreign language)

